                    </div>
                    <div class="progress" id="lo-progress" role="progressbar"
                         aria-valuemin="0" aria-valuemax="100" aria-valuenow="0"
                         aria-label="Late Order Warnings progress">
                        <span class="progress-fill" id="lo-progress-fill"></span>
                    </div>
                    <pre class="console" id="lo-console"></pre>
                </div>
                <p class="note">
                    A group that fails is screenshotted to <code>artifacts/late-orders</code> beside the executable
                    and the run moves on. The one exception is a lost session: if WhatsApp drops back to the QR
                    code the whole run aborts, because every remaining group would fail identically.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="section-title">What happens when you send</div>
            <div class="card">
                <h3>The sequence, per group</h3>
                <p class="sub">
                    Three checks stand between a mapping row and a message a seller can read &mdash; a WhatsApp
                    message cannot be recalled
                </p>
                <div class="table-scroll is-full">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>What it does</th><th>If it fails</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Search</td><td>Clears the box, types the group name, waits for results to settle</td><td>Skips this seller</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Exact title match</strong></td><td>Matches the chat name character for character; requires exactly one hit</td><td>Skips &mdash; never picks one of two</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Header re-check</strong></td><td>Reads the opened chat's title back and compares again, after the click</td><td>Skips before typing anything</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Type</td><td>Whole lines at once, with Shift+Enter for the breaks (Enter would send)</td><td>Retypes key by key, then skips</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Read back</strong></td><td>Reads the composed text out of the box and compares it to what you approved</td><td>Clears the box, skips &mdash; names the differing line</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Send</td><td>Presses Enter, waits for the box to empty</td><td>Warns that it may have gone out</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Pause</td><td>6&ndash;12 s randomised on a live run; 0.7 s on a dry run, which sends nothing to pace</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <p class="note">
                    At most <strong>40 groups per run</strong>; over that the run is refused rather than truncated.
                    Messages are capped at 4,000 characters. A run holds the app-wide automation slot for its whole
                    duration &mdash; roughly 5&ndash;8 minutes for a full batch &mdash; so Create Return cannot run
                    at the same time. The window stays visible on purpose: it is your only way to watch automation
                    touching your own WhatsApp account.
                </p>
            </div>

            <footer class="page-foot">Marketplace Reporting &middot; Late Order Warnings</footer>
        </section>

        @* ================= DATA & METHODOLOGY ================= *@
        <section class="panel" id="panel-methodology" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-methodology" hidden>

            <header class="page-head">
                <div>
                    <h1>Data &amp; Methodology</h1>
                    <p class="lede">
                        Which column every number on these dashboards comes from, how it is calculated, and
                        what the calculation deliberately excludes. The thresholds and code lists on this page
                        are read straight from the code that performs the calculation, so they cannot drift
                        away from what the reports actually do.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <span class="stamp">Reference</span>
            </header>

            <div class="results">

                @* ---------- source ---------- *@

                <div class="section-title">Where the data comes from</div>

                <div class="card">
                    <h3>One file, one upload</h3>
                    <p class="sub">
                        Both report modules read the standard Mirakl <strong>orders export</strong>. Nothing is
                        fetched from an API or a database &mdash; every figure traces back to a cell in the file
                        you upload, and nothing is stored on the server afterwards. The Return SLA module reads
                        two extra return-tracking templates alongside it.
                    </p>
                    <p class="note" style="margin-top:0">
                        The dashboard is computed in your browser. That is why the date filter recalculates every
                        metric instantly &mdash; and why closing the tab discards the data.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="card">
                    <h3>Column by column</h3>
                    <p class="sub">
                        A missing <span class="badge red">required</span> column stops the report with a message
                        naming it. A missing <span class="badge amber">optional</span> column only disables its
                        own section &mdash; everything else still runs
                    </p>
                    <div class="table-scroll is-full">
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Column</th><th>Status</th><th>What it drives</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>Seller</code></td><td><span class="badge red">required</span></td><td>Every per-seller grouping and ranking</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Order number</code></td><td><span class="badge red">required</span></td><td>Row identity; the cancellation detail list</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Date created</code></td><td><span class="badge red">required</span></td><td>The date filter, the volume trend, the start of every duration</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Status</code></td><td><span class="badge red">required</span></td><td>The received / rejected / cancelled counts and rates on <em>Key metrics</em>, refund classification, status mix</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Quantity</code></td><td><span class="badge red">required</span></td><td>Excel <em>Data</em> sheet</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Amount</code></td><td><span class="badge red">required</span></td><td>All revenue figures (line total, VAT included)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Currency</code></td><td><span class="badge red">required</span></td><td>The currency shown on money values</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Shipping deadline</code></td><td><span class="badge red">required</span></td><td>Late / on-time shipment, SLA margin</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Shipping date</code></td><td><span class="badge red">required</span></td><td>Late / on-time shipment, time to ship, transit</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Reason</code></td><td><span class="badge red">required</span></td><td>Detects auto-closed lines; carried into the Excel <em>Data</em> sheet</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Shipping company</code></td><td><span class="badge red">required</span></td><td>Carrier performance, order lines per carrier, and the integrated / manual split</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Received date</code></td><td><span class="badge red">required</span></td><td>Transit time, order-to-delivery time</td></tr>

                                <tr><td><code>Acceptance date</code></td><td><span class="badge amber">optional</span></td><td>Delivery quality &mdash; acceptance time</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Cancellation Request Status</code></td><td><span class="badge amber">optional</span></td><td>Cancellation / rejection / refund breakdown</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Cancellation Request Payload</code></td><td><span class="badge amber">optional</span></td><td>Cancellation reason codes</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Lead time to ship</code></td><td><span class="badge amber">optional</span></td><td>Lead time (SLA) analysis</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Amount transferred to seller (including taxes)</code></td><td><span class="badge amber">optional</span></td><td>Data quality &mdash; payout left on a cancelled line</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Total canceled amount (including taxes)</code></td><td><span class="badge amber">optional</span></td><td>Ranks the line-level table in the cancellation breakdown</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Total refunded amount (including taxes)</code></td><td><span class="badge amber">optional</span></td><td>Ranks the line-level table in the cancellation breakdown</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Order with invoice</code></td><td><span class="badge amber">optional</span></td><td>Invoice coverage, data quality</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Category label</code></td><td><span class="badge amber">optional</span></td><td>Category performance</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Brand</code></td><td><span class="badge amber">optional</span></td><td>Reserved &mdash; sent to the browser but not charted yet</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Shipping address city</code></td><td><span class="badge amber">optional</span></td><td>Reserved &mdash; sent to the browser but not charted yet</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <p class="note">
                    The export writes a corrupted heading over the seller column
                    (<code>1.11111111111111E+052</code> instead of <code>Seller</code>). Fix that one cell before
                    uploading, otherwise the report stops on the required-column check.
                    <strong>Analysis unit:</strong> unless a card says otherwise, every figure counts
                    <em>order lines</em>, not orders &mdash; one order containing three items is three lines.
                </p>

                @* ---------- units ---------- *@

                <div class="section-title">How the numbers are calculated</div>

                <div class="card">
                    <h3>Volume, outcomes and revenue</h3>
                    <p class="sub">
                        The three outcome counts on <em>Key metrics</em> come from the <code>Status</code>
                        column and nothing else &mdash; no date column stands in for a status
                    </p>
                    <div class="table-scroll is-full">
                        <table>
                            <thead>
                                <tr><th>Metric</th><th>What it means</th><th>How it is computed</th><th>Watch out for</th></tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Total order lines</strong></td>
                                    <td>Size of the data set after the date filter.</td>
                                    <td>Row count.</td>
                                    <td>Lines, not orders. It is also the denominator of all three outcome rates below.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Received orders</strong></td>
                                    <td>Lines the customer actually received.</td>
                                    <td><code>Status</code>&nbsp;=&nbsp;@Model.ReceivedStatus.</td>
                                    <td>Lower than the count of lines that carry a shipping date: a parcel that has left the seller but not yet arrived still sits at an in-flight status, and a delivered line that came back is @Model.RefundedStatus.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Rejected orders</strong></td>
                                    <td>Lines the seller turned down.</td>
                                    <td><code>Status</code>&nbsp;=&nbsp;@Model.RejectedStatus.</td>
                                    <td>Different from a cancellation: the order never entered fulfilment at all.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Canceled orders</strong></td>
                                    <td>Lines cancelled before delivery, whoever asked for it.</td>
                                    <td><code>Status</code>&nbsp;=&nbsp;@Model.CanceledStatus.</td>
                                    <td>Who caused it is not in the status &mdash; see the outcome breakdown further down.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Gross revenue</strong></td>
                                    <td>Sales value on a group of lines, VAT included.</td>
                                    <td>Sum of <code>Amount</code>.</td>
                                    <td>Cancelled lines carry <code>Amount</code>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0, so they drop out on their own. A refunded line does <em>not</em> &mdash; see the traps below.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Lost amount</strong></td>
                                    <td>Revenue lost to cancellations and refunds.</td>
                                    <td><code>Total canceled amount</code>&nbsp;+&nbsp;<code>Total refunded amount</code>.</td>
                                    <td>Money-weighted, not count-weighted: one expensive cancellation moves it a lot. Not printed anywhere as a figure &mdash; it is what ranks <em>Every line that did not complete cleanly</em>, worst first.</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="card">
                    <h3>Shipping, deadlines and delivery</h3>
                    <div class="table-scroll is-full">
                        <table>
                            <thead>
                                <tr><th>Metric</th><th>What it means</th><th>How it is computed</th><th>Watch out for</th></tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Late shipment</strong></td>
                                    <td>The promised dispatch deadline was missed.</td>
                                    <td><code>Shipping date</code> is filled in <em>and</em> later than <code>Shipping deadline</code>.</td>
                                    <td>Lines not yet shipped are excluded from the denominator entirely &mdash; they are not counted as on time.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>On-time shipment rate</strong></td>
                                    <td>Share of dispatches that met the deadline.</td>
                                    <td>Lines where <code>Shipping date</code>&nbsp;&le;&nbsp;<code>Shipping deadline</code>, over all shipped lines.</td>
                                    <td>Same denominator rule as above.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Time to ship</strong></td>
                                    <td>How long the order took to leave the seller.</td>
                                    <td><code>Shipping date</code>&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;<code>Date created</code>, in hours.</td>
                                    <td>Measured from the order, not from acceptance, so it is comparable with the deadline.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Acceptance time</strong></td>
                                    <td>How long the seller took to acknowledge the order.</td>
                                    <td><code>Acceptance date</code>&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;<code>Date created</code>, in hours.</td>
                                    <td>Blank acceptance date means the seller never accepted &mdash; flagged in data quality.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Transit time</strong></td>
                                    <td>Time spent with the carrier.</td>
                                    <td><code>Received date</code>&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;<code>Shipping date</code>.</td>
                                    <td><strong>Excludes auto-closed lines</strong> (see below). The count each average rests on is shown as <em>measured deliveries</em>.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Order-to-delivery</strong></td>
                                    <td>End-to-end wait from the customer's point of view.</td>
                                    <td><code>Received date</code>&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;<code>Date created</code>.</td>
                                    <td>Also excludes auto-closed lines.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Integrated vs. manual carrier</strong></td>
                                    <td>Whether delivery confirmation arrives automatically.</td>
                                    <td><code>Shipping company</code> contains any of: @Model.CarrierKeywordList (case-insensitive).</td>
                                    <td>A manual carrier's delivery time reflects when somebody ticked the box, not when the parcel arrived.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Best / worst on-time sellers</strong></td>
                                    <td>Sellers ranked by deadline performance.</td>
                                    <td>On-time rate per seller, minimum <strong>@Model.MinSampleSize shipped lines</strong> to be ranked.</td>
                                    <td>The minimum exists so a seller with one lucky order does not top the list.</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="card">
                    <h3>Cancellation, rejection and refund</h3>
                    <p class="sub">
                        <code>Status</code> on its own cannot separate these three, which is the whole reason this
                        breakdown exists. Crossing it with the cancellation request outcome can
                    </p>
                    <div class="table-scroll is-full">
                        <table>
                            <thead>
                                <tr><th>Outcome</th><th>Rule</th><th>Why it is worth separating</th></tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><span class="badge">Completed normally</span></td>
                                    <td>Not cancelled, not refunded, no request on record.</td>
                                    <td>The healthy baseline.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><span class="badge amber">Customer cancellation</span></td>
                                    <td><code>Status</code>&nbsp;=&nbsp;@Model.CanceledStatus <em>and</em> request status ACCEPTED.</td>
                                    <td>Demand-side loss. Its reason code tells you what to fix.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><span class="badge red">Seller / operator cancellation</span></td>
                                    <td><code>Status</code>&nbsp;=&nbsp;@Model.CanceledStatus <em>and no request on record</em>.</td>
                                    <td>Supply-side failure &mdash; stock-out or pricing error. Belongs on the seller scorecard, not in customer-behaviour reporting.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><span class="badge green">Cancellation request rejected</span></td>
                                    <td>Request status REJECTED and the line is not cancelled.</td>
                                    <td>Revenue that was saved. Invisible in the status column, because the order looks completely normal.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><span class="badge red">Refunded after delivery</span></td>
                                    <td><code>Status</code>&nbsp;=&nbsp;@Model.RefundedStatus.</td>
                                    <td>Different problem from a cancellation: the goods shipped, arrived, and came back.</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                    <p class="note">
                        <strong>Reason codes</strong> are lifted out of the <code>Cancellation Request Payload</code>
                        JSON. Only the code is read; the customer's free-text note stays in the file and is never
                        sent to the browser. The wording below is our mapping of the standard Mirakl codes and is
                        worth confirming with the platform:
                    </p>
                    <div class="table-scroll is-full">
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Reading</th><th>Suggested follow-up</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                @foreach (var reason in Model.ReasonLabels)
                                {
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><span class="badge">@reason.Code</span></td>
                                        <td>@reason.Label</td>
                                        <td>@reason.Action</td>
                                    </tr>
                                }
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="card">
                    <h3>Lead time (SLA)</h3>
                    <div class="table-scroll is-full">
                        <table>
                            <thead>
                                <tr><th>Metric</th><th>What it means</th><th>How it is computed</th><th>Watch out for</th></tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Promised lead time</strong></td>
                                    <td>Days the seller committed to for dispatch.</td>
                                    <td><code>Lead time to ship</code>, averaged across all of a seller&rsquo;s shipped lines (so a promise used on more lines weighs more).</td>
                                    <td>A seller can promise different windows on different products &mdash; the <em>promise range</em> column shows the spread the average came from.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>SLA usage</strong></td>
                                    <td>How much of that window is actually consumed. The most actionable number on the dashboard.</td>
                                    <td>Time to ship&nbsp;&divide;&nbsp;(promised days&nbsp;&times;&nbsp;24).</td>
                                    <td>100% = exactly on time. Below 50% means the promise is far wider than reality. Above 100% means the deadline is being missed. Same-day (0-day) promises are excluded, since dividing by zero has no meaning.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Suggested lead time</strong></td>
                                    <td>A promise the seller could actually keep.</td>
                                    <td>Round up (90th percentile of time to ship&nbsp;&divide;&nbsp;24).</td>
                                    <td><strong>The 90th percentile, not the average</strong>, so slow days stay inside the promise. An average-based suggestion would be missed roughly half the time.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Days to gain</strong></td>
                                    <td>How much the promise could be shortened.</td>
                                    <td>Promised&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;suggested, listed only when it is at least a full day.</td>
                                    <td>One row per seller, over all their shipped lines, with a minimum of <strong>@Model.MinLeadTimeSample shipped lines</strong>. A seller whose promise range is wide (2 days on accessories, 15 on white goods) gets a single blended suggestion &mdash; take it to them per product group rather than applying it to every listing.</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                </div>

                @* ---------- traps ---------- *@

                <div class="section-title">What the export gets wrong</div>

                <div class="card accent-red">
                    <h3>Traps handled on your behalf</h3>
                    <p class="sub">
                        These are properties of the Mirakl export, not of this app. Each one quietly corrupts a
                        total if the column is summed as-is
                    </p>
                    <div class="table-scroll is-full">
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Trap</th><th>What actually happens</th><th>What the report does</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><span class="badge red">Phantom payout</span></td>
                                    <td><code>Amount transferred to seller</code> is not cleared when a line is cancelled &mdash; it keeps showing what would have been paid.</td>
                                    <td>Never summed as a headline figure. <em>Data quality</em> counts the cancelled lines that still carry a payout and names the amount, so the overstatement is visible before anyone sums the column for settlement.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><span class="badge red">Refunds keep their revenue</span></td>
                                    <td>A cancellation zeroes <code>Amount</code>; a refund does not. So a refund does not reduce revenue on its own.</td>
                                    <td>Refunded lines are a separate outcome in the breakdown, and the line-level table is ranked by <code>Total refunded amount</code> rather than by <code>Amount</code> &mdash; which would put them last, as if nothing had been lost.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><span class="badge amber">Auto-closed deliveries</span></td>
                                    <td>Lines whose <code>Reason</code> is &ldquo;@Model.AutoReceivedReason&rdquo; were closed by the platform with a bulk timestamp because the carrier never reported a delivery.</td>
                                    <td>Excluded from every duration in <em>Delivery quality</em>. The card next to it measures that cohort on its own, so the gap against the real-delivery average shows how far they would drag it &mdash; that gap is the size of the distortion.</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                    <p class="note">
                        The <em>Avg. hours to receive</em> cards under <strong>Key metrics</strong> are the original
                        ported metric and do <strong>not</strong> exclude auto-closed lines. That is intentional:
                        those numbers were signed off against the previous report and must keep matching it. Use
                        the <em>Delivery quality</em> cards when you want the corrected view.
                    </p>
                </div>

                @* ---------- return module ---------- *@

                <div class="section-title">Return SLA module</div>

                <div class="card">
                    <h3>Return rules</h3>
                    <div class="table-scroll is-full">
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>SLA window</strong></td>
                                    <td><strong>@Model.ReturnSlaDays days</strong> from the date the return was shipped back to the seller, with an early warning at <strong>@Model.ReturnWarningDays days</strong>.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>What counts as shipped back</strong></td>
                                    <td>Template A: <code>Kargo Takip Kodu</code> is filled in. Template B: <code>YK Takip Kodu</code> is filled in.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>SLA start date</strong></td>
                                    <td>Template B uses <code>Kargo Kodu Oluşturma Tarihi</code>. Template A has no ship-date column at all, so <code>Talep Tarihi</code> (request date) stands in as the closest available proxy &mdash; which makes template A elapsed times slightly conservative.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Matching</strong></td>
                                    <td>Return rows are matched to the orders export by order number. Rows with no match are labelled rather than dropped.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Refund time</strong></td>
                                    <td>Order created &rarr; customer debit date, computed over the whole orders file rather than only the rows present in the templates.</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                </div>

                @* ---------- limits ---------- *@

                <div class="section-title">What this report cannot tell you</div>

                <div class="card accent-navy">
                    <h3>Limits</h3>
                    <div class="table-scroll is-full">
                        <table>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Rates on small samples</strong></td>
                                    <td>A seller with two orders can show a 50% cancellation rate. Minimum sample sizes are applied to the ranked lists (@Model.MinSampleSize lines) and to lead-time advice (@Model.MinLeadTimeSample lines), but the per-seller tables show everyone &mdash; read the line count next to the rate.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Delivery times</strong></td>
                                    <td>Only cover lines with a genuine carrier delivery notification. Where a carrier does not report back, the line is counted as auto-closed and left out.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Time zones</strong></td>
                                    <td>Timestamps are used exactly as they appear in the export. No time-zone conversion is applied anywhere.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Reason code wording</strong></td>
                                    <td>Our reading of the standard codes, not an official mapping. Worth confirming with the platform before acting on it.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Accounting</strong></td>
                                    <td>Figures are the export's own. The reconciliation proves the file is internally consistent &mdash; it is not a substitute for reconciling against the ledger.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Trends</strong></td>
                                    <td>The volume trend is only meaningful when the export spans several days. A single-day export produces a single point.</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <footer class="page-foot">Marketplace Reporting &middot; Data &amp; Methodology</footer>
            </div>
        </section>

    </main>
</div>

@section Scripts {
    <script src="~/lib/chartjs/chart.umd.js" asp-append-version="true"></script>
    <script src="~/js/app.js" asp-append-version="true"></script>
    <script src="~/js/order-report.js" asp-append-version="true"></script>
    <script src="~/js/return-sla-report.js" asp-append-version="true"></script>
    <script src="~/js/create-return.js" asp-append-version="true"></script>
    <script src="~/js/late-orders.js" asp-append-version="true"></script>
}
